    {
      "name": "each of the two standings answers on its own",
      "law": "The judgment",
      "obligations": ["XII-3", "VIII-1"],
      "derivation": "The first of the two voices asks an ordinary question. It is found as a current holder in the inbound record, which Article XII step 4 calls an ask — \"Found as a current holder in the **inbound** record → an ask\" — and the ambiguity below is nowhere near it, because a current holder is matched on the voice itself and never on a hash. **This exchange is here so the refusal below cannot be read as a broken door.** Two standings sharing one heir commitment is a legal state a granter can reach, and the door works normally in it: this voice is answered, its mark moves, and the other row is untouched. A kit that refused everything once it held two such rows would pass the case beneath and fail here.",
